<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Amphipolis</h1><p class="st-meta">437 BCE – 357 BCE · capital Amphipolis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Athenian colony on the Strymon river, key to the timber and gold of Thrace, whose loss to Sparta in 424 BCE exiled the historian Thucydides.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The site, a loop of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Strymon</a> called Ennea Hodoi (Nine Ways), had long attracted <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>; a first attempt to settle it ended in disaster around 465 BCE. In 437 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagnon">Hagnon</a> founded Amphipolis there with Athenian and allied settlers, controlling the river crossing and the route to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-pangaion">Mount Pangaion</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Amphipolis was a mixed community in which Athenians were a minority among other Greek settlers, which weakened loyalty to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>. It governed itself through an assembly and magistrates, honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagnon">Hagnon</a> as founder and later the Spartan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasidas">Brasidas</a>, and grew rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> for shipbuilding, precious metals and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasidas">Brasidas</a> captured it in 424 BCE; both he and the Athenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleon">Cleon</a> died in battle there in 422 BCE. Independent afterwards and never recovered by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>, it fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> in 357 BCE. It became a leading Macedonian city and mint, and in 167 BCE capital of one of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/four-macedonian-republics">Four Macedonian Republics</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/amphipolis.jpg" alt="Amphipolis" width="320" height="431"><figcaption>Image: OpenStreetMap contributors and FocalPoint · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kasta_tumulus_and_Lion_of_Amphipolis_location_map_en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>437 BCE – 357 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amphipolis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ennea Hodoi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-397&amp;focus=amphipolis" aria-label="Amphipolis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5264 720 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Amphipolis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5584 976L5591 1068M5584 976L5548 987"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0M5548 987h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5584 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5584 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-397&amp;focus=amphipolis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
